(By P.T. Barnum) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the United States, where we have more land than people, it is not) Tj T*
(at all difficult for persons in good health to make money. In this) Tj T*
(comparatively new field there are so many avenues of success open, so) Tj T*
(many vocations which are not crowded, that any person of either sex who) Tj T*
(is willing, at least for the time being, to engage in any respectable) Tj T*
(occupation that offers, may find lucrative employment.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Those who really desire to attain an independence, have only to set) Tj T*
(their minds upon it, and adopt the proper means, as they do in regard to) Tj T*
(any other object which they wish to accomplish, and the thing is easily) Tj T*
(done. But however easy it may be found to make money, I have no doubt) Tj T*
(many of my hearers will agree it is the most difficult thing in the) Tj T*
(world to keep it. The road to wealth is, as Dr. Franklin truly says,) Tj T*
("as plain as the road to the mill." It consists simply in expending less) Tj T*
(than we earn; that seems to be a very simple problem. Mr. Micawber,) Tj T*
(one of those happy creations of the genial Dickens, puts the case in a) Tj T*
(strong light when he says that to have annual income of twenty pounds) Tj T*
(per annum, and spend twenty pounds and sixpence, is to be the most) Tj T*
(miserable of men; whereas, to have an income of only twenty pounds, and) Tj T*
(spend but nineteen pounds and sixpence is to be the happiest of mortals.) Tj T*
(Many of my readers may say, "we understand this: this is economy, and we) Tj T*
(know economy is wealth; we know we can't eat our cake and keep it also.") Tj T*
(Yet I beg to say that perhaps more cases of failure arise from mistakes) Tj T*
(on this point than almost any other. The fact is, many people think they) Tj T*
(understand economy when they really do not.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(True economy is misapprehended, and people go through life without) Tj T*
(properly comprehending what that principle is. One says, "I have an) Tj T*
(income of so much, and here is my neighbor who has the same; yet every) Tj T*
(year he gets something ahead and I fall short; why is it? I know all) Tj T*
(about economy." He thinks he does, but he does not. There are men who) Tj T*
(think that economy consists in saving cheese-parings and candle-ends,) Tj T*
(in cutting off two pence from the laundress' bill and doing all sorts of) Tj T*
(little, mean, dirty things. Economy is not meanness. The misfortune is,) Tj T*
(also, that this class of persons let their economy apply in only one) Tj T*
(direction. They fancy they are so wonderfully economical in saving a) Tj T*
(half-penny where they ought to spend twopence, that they think they can) Tj T*
(afford to squander in other directions. A few years ago, before kerosene) Tj T*
(oil was discovered or thought of, one might stop overnight at almost any) Tj T*
(farmer's house in the agricultural districts and get a very good supper,) Tj T*
(but after supper he might attempt to read in the sitting-room, and) Tj T*
(would find it impossible with the inefficient light of one candle. The) Tj T*
(hostess, seeing his dilemma, would say: "It is rather difficult to read) Tj T*
(here evenings; the proverb says 'you must have a ship at sea in order) Tj T*
(to be able to burn two candles at once;' we never have an extra candle) Tj T*
(except on extra occasions." These extra occasions occur, perhaps, twice) Tj T*
(a year. In this way the good woman saves five, six, or ten dollars in) Tj T*
(that time: but the information which might be derived from having the) Tj T*
