(extra light would, of course, far outweigh a ton of candles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But the trouble does not end here. Feeling that she is so economical) Tj T*
(in tallow candies, she thinks she can afford to go frequently to the) Tj T*
(village and spend twenty or thirty dollars for ribbons and furbelows,) Tj T*
(many of which are not necessary. This false connote may frequently) Tj T*
(be seen in men of business, and in those instances it often runs to) Tj T*
(writing-paper. You find good businessmen who save all the old envelopes) Tj T*
(and scraps, and would not tear a new sheet of paper, if they could avoid) Tj T*
(it, for the world. This is all very well; they may in this way save five) Tj T*
(or ten dollars a year, but being so economical \(only in note paper\),) Tj T*
(they think they can afford to waste time; to have expensive parties,) Tj T*
(and to drive their carriages. This is an illustration of Dr. Franklin's) Tj T*
("saving at the spigot and wasting at the bung-hole;" "penny wise and) Tj T*
(pound foolish." Punch in speaking of this "one idea" class of people) Tj T*
(says "they are like the man who bought a penny herring for his family's) Tj T*
(dinner and then hired a coach and four to take it home." I never knew a) Tj T*
(man to succeed by practising this kind of economy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(True economy consists in always making the income exceed the out-go.) Tj T*
(Wear the old clothes a little longer if necessary; dispense with the new) Tj T*
(pair of gloves; mend the old dress: live on plainer food if need be; so) Tj T*
(that, under all circumstances, unless some unforeseen accident occurs,) Tj T*
(there will be a margin in favor of the income. A penny here, and a) Tj T*
(dollar there, placed at interest, goes on accumulating, and in this way) Tj T*
(the desired result is attained. It requires some training, perhaps, to) Tj T*
(accomplish this economy, but when once used to it, you will find there) Tj T*
(is more satisfaction in rational saving than in irrational spending.) Tj T*
(Here is a recipe which I recommend: I have found it to work an excellent) Tj T*
(cure for extravagance, and especially for mistaken economy: When you) Tj T*
(find that you have no surplus at the end of the year, and yet have a) Tj T*
(good income, I advise you to take a few sheets of paper and form them) Tj T*
(into a book and mark down every item of expenditure. Post it every day) Tj T*
(or week in two columns, one headed "necessaries" or even "comforts", and) Tj T*
(the other headed "luxuries," and you will find that the latter column) Tj T*
(will be double, treble, and frequently ten times greater than the) Tj T*
(former. The real comforts of life cost but a small portion of what most) Tj T*
(of us can earn. Dr. Franklin says "it is the eyes of others and not) Tj T*
(our own eyes which ruin us. If all the world were blind except myself I) Tj T*
(should not care for fine clothes or furniture." It is the fear of what) Tj T*
(Mrs. Grundy may say that keeps the noses of many worthy families to the) Tj T*
(grindstone. In America many persons like to repeat "we are all free and) Tj T*
(equal," but it is a great mistake in more senses than one.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That we are born "free and equal" is a glorious truth in one sense, yet) Tj T*
(we are not all born equally rich, and we never shall be. One may say;) Tj T*
("there is a man who has an income of fifty thousand dollars per annum,) Tj T*
(while I have but one thousand dollars; I knew that fellow when he was) Tj T*
(poor like myself; now he is rich and thinks he is better than I am; I) Tj T*
(will show him that I am as good as he is; I will go and buy a horse and) Tj T*
(buggy; no, I cannot do that, but I will go and hire one and ride this) Tj T*
(afternoon on the same road that he does, and thus prove to him that I am) Tj T*
