(as good as he is.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(My friend, you need not take that trouble; you can easily prove that you) Tj T*
(are "as good as he is;" you have only to behave as well as he does; but) Tj T*
(you cannot make anybody believe that you are rich as he is. Besides, if) Tj T*
(you put on these "airs," add waste your time and spend your money, your) Tj T*
(poor wife will be obliged to scrub her fingers off at home, and buy her) Tj T*
(tea two ounces at a time, and everything else in proportion, in order) Tj T*
(that you may keep up "appearances," and, after all, deceive nobody. On) Tj T*
(the other hand, Mrs. Smith may say that her next-door neighbor) Tj T*
(married Johnson for his money, and "everybody says so." She has a nice) Tj T*
(one-thousand dollar camel's hair shawl, and she will make Smith get her) Tj T*
(an imitation one, and she will sit in a pew right next to her neighbor) Tj T*
(in church, in order to prove that she is her equal.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(My good woman, you will not get ahead in the world, if your vanity and) Tj T*
(envy thus take the lead. In this country, where we believe the majority) Tj T*
(ought to rule, we ignore that principle in regard to fashion, and let) Tj T*
(a handful of people, calling themselves the aristocracy, run up a false) Tj T*
(standard of perfection, and in endeavoring to rise to that standard, we) Tj T*
(constantly keep ourselves poor; all the time digging away for the sake) Tj T*
(of outside appearances. How much wiser to be a "law unto ourselves" and) Tj T*
(say, "we will regulate our out-go by our income, and lay up something) Tj T*
(for a rainy day." People ought to be as sensible on the subject of) Tj T*
(money-getting as on any other subject. Like causes produces like) Tj T*
(effects. You cannot accumulate a fortune by taking the road that leads) Tj T*
(to poverty. It needs no prophet to tell us that those who live fully up) Tj T*
(to their means, without any thought of a reverse in this life, can never) Tj T*
(attain a pecuniary independence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Men and women accustomed to gratify every whim and caprice, will find it) Tj T*
(hard, at first, to cut down their various unnecessary expenses, and will) Tj T*
(feel it a great self-denial to live in a smaller house than they have) Tj T*
(been accustomed to, with less expensive furniture, less company, less) Tj T*
(costly clothing, fewer servants, a less number of balls, parties,) Tj T*
(theater-goings, carriage-ridings, pleasure excursions, cigar-smokings,) Tj T*
(liquor-drinkings, and other extravagances; but, after all, if they will) Tj T*
(try the plan of laying by a "nest-egg," or, in other words, a small) Tj T*
(sum of money, at interest or judiciously invested in land, they will be) Tj T*
(surprised at the pleasure to be derived from constantly adding to their) Tj T*
(little "pile," as well as from all the economical habits which are) Tj T*
(engendered by this course.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The old suit of clothes, and the old bonnet and dress, will answer for) Tj T*
(another season; the Croton or spring water taste better than champagne;) Tj T*
(a cold bath and a brisk walk will prove more exhilarating than a ride) Tj T*
(in the finest coach; a social chat, an evening's reading in the family) Tj T*
(circle, or an hour's play of "hunt the slipper" and "blind man's buff") Tj T*
(will be far more pleasant than a fifty or five hundred dollar party,) Tj T*
(when the reflection on the difference in cost is indulged in by those) Tj T*
(who begin to know the pleasures of saving. Thousands of men are kept) Tj T*
(poor, and tens of thousands are made so after they have acquired quite) Tj T*
