(sufficient to support them well through life, in consequence of laying) Tj T*
(their plans of living on too broad a platform. Some families expend) Tj T*
(twenty thousand dollars per annum, and some much more, and would) Tj T*
(scarcely know how to live on less, while others secure more solid) Tj T*
(enjoyment frequently on a twentieth part of that amount. Prosperity is) Tj T*
(a more severe ordeal than adversity, especially sudden prosperity.) Tj T*
("Easy come, easy go," is an old and true proverb. A spirit of pride and) Tj T*
(vanity, when permitted to have full sway, is the undying canker-worm) Tj T*
(which gnaws the very vitals of a man's worldly possessions, let them be) Tj T*
(small or great, hundreds, or millions. Many persons, as they begin) Tj T*
(to prosper, immediately expand their ideas and commence expending for) Tj T*
(luxuries, until in a short time their expenses swallow up their) Tj T*
(income, and they become ruined in their ridiculous attempts to keep up) Tj T*
(appearances, and make a "sensation.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I know a gentleman of fortune who says, that when he first began to) Tj T*
(prosper, his wife would have a new and elegant sofa. "That sofa," he) Tj T*
(says, "cost me thirty thousand dollars!" When the sofa reached the) Tj T*
(house, it was found necessary to get chairs to match; then side-boards,) Tj T*
(carpets and tables "to correspond" with them, and so on through the) Tj T*
(entire stock of furniture; when at last it was found that the house) Tj T*
(itself was quite too small and old-fashioned for the furniture, and a) Tj T*
(new one was built to correspond with the new purchases; "thus," added my) Tj T*
(friend, "summing up an outlay of thirty thousand dollars, caused by that) Tj T*
(single sofa, and saddling on me, in the shape of servants, equipage, and) Tj T*
(the necessary expenses attendant upon keeping up a fine 'establishment,') Tj T*
(a yearly outlay of eleven thousand dollars, and a tight pinch at that:) Tj T*
(whereas, ten years ago, we lived with much more real comfort, because) Tj T*
(with much less care, on as many hundreds. The truth is," he continued,) Tj T*
("that sofa would have brought me to inevitable bankruptcy, had not a) Tj T*
(most unexampled title to prosperity kept me above it, and had I not) Tj T*
(checked the natural desire to 'cut a dash'.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The foundation of success in life is good health: that is the substratum) Tj T*
(fortune; it is also the basis of happiness. A person cannot accumulate a) Tj T*
(fortune very well when he is sick. He has no ambition; no incentive; no) Tj T*
(force. Of course, there are those who have bad health and cannot help) Tj T*
(it: you cannot expect that such persons can accumulate wealth, but there) Tj T*
(are a great many in poor health who need not be so.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If, then, sound health is the foundation of success and happiness in) Tj T*
(life, how important it is that we should study the laws of health, which) Tj T*
(is but another expression for the laws of nature! The nearer we keep to) Tj T*
(the laws of nature, the nearer we are to good health, and yet how many) Tj T*
(persons there are who pay no attention to natural laws, but absolutely) Tj T*
(transgress them, even against their own natural inclination. We ought) Tj T*
(to know that the "sin of ignorance" is never winked at in regard to the) Tj T*
(violation of nature's laws; their infraction always brings the penalty.) Tj T*
(A child may thrust its finger into the flames without knowing it will) Tj T*
(burn, and so suffers, repentance, even, will not stop the smart. Many of) Tj T*
(our ancestors knew very little about the principle of ventilation. They) Tj T*
(did not know much about oxygen, whatever other "gin" they might have) Tj T*
