(Many persons knowingly violate the laws of nature against their better) Tj T*
(impulses, for the sake of fashion. For instance, there is one thing) Tj T*
(that nothing living except a vile worm ever naturally loved, and that) Tj T*
(is tobacco; yet how many persons there are who deliberately train an) Tj T*
(unnatural appetite, and overcome this implanted aversion for tobacco,) Tj T*
(to such a degree that they get to love it. They have got hold of a) Tj T*
(poisonous, filthy weed, or rather that takes a firm hold of them. Here) Tj T*
(are married men who run about spitting tobacco juice on the carpet and) Tj T*
(floors, and sometimes even upon their wives besides. They do not kick) Tj T*
(their wives out of doors like drunken men, but their wives, I have) Tj T*
(no doubt, often wish they were outside of the house. Another perilous) Tj T*
(feature is that this artificial appetite, like jealousy, "grows by what) Tj T*
(it feeds on;" when you love that which is unnatural, a stronger appetite) Tj T*
(is created for the hurtful thing than the natural desire for what is) Tj T*
(harmless. There is an old proverb which says that "habit is second) Tj T*
(nature," but an artificial habit is stronger than nature. Take for) Tj T*
(instance, an old tobacco-chewer; his love for the "quid" is stronger) Tj T*
(than his love for any particular kind of food. He can give up roast beef) Tj T*
(easier than give up the weed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Young lads regret that they are not men; they would like to go to bed) Tj T*
(boys and wake up men; and to accomplish this they copy the bad habits of) Tj T*
(their seniors. Little Tommy and Johnny see their fathers or uncles smoke) Tj T*
(a pipe, and they say, "If I could only do that, I would be a man too;) Tj T*
(uncle John has gone out and left his pipe of tobacco, let us try it.") Tj T*
(They take a match and light it, and then puff away. "We will learn to) Tj T*
(smoke; do you like it Johnny?" That lad dolefully replies: "Not very) Tj T*
(much; it tastes bitter;" by and by he grows pale, but he persists and he) Tj T*
(soon offers up a sacrifice on the altar of fashion; but the boys stick) Tj T*
(to it and persevere until at last they conquer their natural appetites) Tj T*
(and become the victims of acquired tastes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I speak "by the book," for I have noticed its effects on myself, having) Tj T*
(gone so far as to smoke ten or fifteen cigars a day; although I have not) Tj T*
(used the weed during the last fourteen years, and never shall again.) Tj T*
(The more a man smokes, the more he craves smoking; the last cigar smoked) Tj T*
(simply excites the desire for another, and so on incessantly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Take the tobacco-chewer. In the morning, when he gets up, he puts a quid) Tj T*
(in his mouth and keeps it there all day, never taking it out except to) Tj T*
(exchange it for a fresh one, or when he is going to eat; oh! yes, at) Tj T*
(intervals during the day and evening, many a chewer takes out the quid) Tj T*
(and holds it in his hand long enough to take a drink, and then pop it) Tj T*
(goes back again. This simply proves that the appetite for rum is even) Tj T*
