(large sums in this way \(we seldom hear of the losers\), and, like other) Tj T*
(speculators, he "looks for his money where he loses it." He tries again.) Tj T*
(endorsing notes has become chronic with you, and at every loss he gets) Tj T*
(your signature for whatever amount he wants. Finally you discover) Tj T*
(your friend has lost all of his property and all of yours. You are) Tj T*
(overwhelmed with astonishment and grief, and you say "it is a hard) Tj T*
(thing; my friend here has ruined me," but, you should add, "I have also) Tj T*
(ruined him." If you had said in the first place, "I will accommodate) Tj T*
(you, but I never indorse without taking ample security," he could not) Tj T*
(have gone beyond the length of his tether, and he would never have been) Tj T*
(tempted away from his legitimate business. It is a very dangerous) Tj T*
(thing, therefore, at any time, to let people get possession of money) Tj T*
(too easily; it tempts them to hazardous speculations, if nothing more.) Tj T*
(Solomon truly said "he that hateth suretiship is sure.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(So with the young man starting in business; let him understand the value) Tj T*
(of money by earning it. When he does understand its value, then grease) Tj T*
(the wheels a little in helping him to start business, but remember, men) Tj T*
(who get money with too great facility cannot usually succeed. You must) Tj T*
(get the first dollars by hard knocks, and at some sacrifice, in order to) Tj T*
(appreciate the value of those dollars.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
