(ADVERTISE YOUR BUSINESS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We all depend, more or less, upon the public for our support. We) Tj T*
(all trade with the public--lawyers, doctors, shoemakers, artists,) Tj T*
(blacksmiths, showmen, opera stagers, railroad presidents, and college) Tj T*
(professors. Those who deal with the public must be careful that their) Tj T*
(goods are valuable; that they are genuine, and will give satisfaction.) Tj T*
(When you get an article which you know is going to please your) Tj T*
(customers, and that when they have tried it, they will feel they have) Tj T*
(got their money's worth, then let the fact be known that you have got) Tj T*
(it. Be careful to advertise it in some shape or other because it is) Tj T*
(evident that if a man has ever so good an article for sale, and nobody) Tj T*
(knows it, it will bring him no return. In a country like this, where) Tj T*
(nearly everybody reads, and where newspapers are issued and circulated) Tj T*
(in editions of five thousand to two hundred thousand, it would be very) Tj T*
(unwise if this channel was not taken advantage of to reach the public in) Tj T*
(advertising. A newspaper goes into the family, and is read by wife and) Tj T*
(children, as well as the head of the home; hence hundreds and thousands) Tj T*
(of people may read your advertisement, while you are attending to your) Tj T*
(routine business. Many, perhaps, read it while you are asleep. The whole) Tj T*
(philosophy of life is, first "sow," then "reap." That is the way the) Tj T*
(farmer does; he plants his potatoes and corn, and sows his grain, and) Tj T*
(then goes about something else, and the time comes when he reaps. But) Tj T*
(he never reaps first and sows afterwards. This principle applies to all) Tj T*
(kinds of business, and to nothing more eminently than to advertising. If) Tj T*
(a man has a genuine article, there is no way in which he can reap more) Tj T*
(advantageously than by "sowing" to the public in this way. He must,) Tj T*
(of course, have a really good article, and one which will please his) Tj T*
(customers; anything spurious will not succeed permanently because the) Tj T*
(public is wiser than many imagine. Men and women are selfish, and we all) Tj T*
(prefer purchasing where we can get the most for our money and we try to) Tj T*
(find out where we can most surely do so.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(You may advertise a spurious article, and induce many people to call and) Tj T*
(buy it once, but they will denounce you as an impostor and swindler, and) Tj T*
(your business will gradually die out and leave you poor. This is right.) Tj T*
(Few people can safely depend upon chance custom. You all need to have) Tj T*
(your customers return and purchase again. A man said to me, "I have) Tj T*
(tried advertising and did not succeed; yet I have a good article.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I replied, "My friend, there may be exceptions to a general rule. But) Tj T*
(how do you advertise?") Tj T*
() Tj T*
("I put it in a weekly newspaper three times, and paid a dollar and a) Tj T*
(half for it." I replied: "Sir, advertising is like learning--'a little) Tj T*
(is a dangerous thing!'") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A French writer says that "The reader of a newspaper does not see the) Tj T*
(first mention of an ordinary advertisement; the second insertion he) Tj T*
(sees, but does not read; the third insertion he reads; the fourth) Tj T*
(insertion, he looks at the price; the fifth insertion, he speaks of) Tj T*
(it to his wife; the sixth insertion, he is ready to purchase, and the) Tj T*
