(seventh insertion, he purchases." Your object in advertising is to make) Tj T*
(the public understand what you have got to sell, and if you have not the) Tj T*
(pluck to keep advertising, until you have imparted that information, all) Tj T*
(the money you have spent is lost. You are like the fellow who told the) Tj T*
(gentleman if he would give him ten cents it would save him a dollar.) Tj T*
("How can I help you so much with so small a sum?" asked the gentleman) Tj T*
(in surprise. "I started out this morning \(hiccuped the fellow\) with) Tj T*
(the full determination to get drunk, and I have spent my only dollar) Tj T*
(to accomplish the object, and it has not quite done it. Ten cents worth) Tj T*
(more of whiskey would just do it, and in this manner I should save the) Tj T*
(dollar already expended.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(So a man who advertises at all must keep it up until the public know who) Tj T*
(and what he is, and what his business is, or else the money invested in) Tj T*
(advertising is lost.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some men have a peculiar genius for writing a striking advertisement,) Tj T*
(one that will arrest the attention of the reader at first sight. This) Tj T*
(fact, of course, gives the advertiser a great advantage. Sometimes a) Tj T*
(man makes himself popular by an unique sign or a curious display in his) Tj T*
(window, recently I observed a swing sign extending over the sidewalk in) Tj T*
(front of a store, on which was the inscription in plain letters,) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
("DON'T READ THE OTHER SIDE") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of course I did, and so did everybody else, and I learned that the man) Tj T*
(had made all independence by first attracting the public to his business) Tj T*
(in that way and then using his customers well afterwards.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Genin, the hatter, bought the first Jenny Lind ticket at auction for) Tj T*
(two hundred and twenty-five dollars, because he knew it would be a good) Tj T*
(advertisement for him. "Who is the bidder?" said the auctioneer, as he) Tj T*
(knocked down that ticket at Castle Garden. "Genin, the hatter," was the) Tj T*
(response. Here were thousands of people from the Fifth avenue, and from) Tj T*
(distant cities in the highest stations in life. "Who is 'Genin,' the) Tj T*
(hatter?" they exclaimed. They had never heard of him before. The next) Tj T*
(morning the newspapers and telegraph had circulated the facts from Maine) Tj T*
(to Texas, and from five to ten millions off people had read that the) Tj T*
(tickets sold at auction For Jenny Lind's first concert amounted to) Tj T*
(about twenty thousand dollars, and that a single ticket was sold at two) Tj T*
(hundred and twenty-five dollars, to "Genin, the hatter." Men throughout) Tj T*
(the country involuntarily took off their hats to see if they had a) Tj T*
("Genin" hat on their heads. At a town in Iowa it was found that in the) Tj T*
(crowd around the post office, there was one man who had a "Genin" hat,) Tj T*
(and he showed it in triumph, although it was worn out and not worth two) Tj T*
(cents. "Why," one man exclaimed, "you have a real 'Genin' hat; what a) Tj T*
(lucky fellow you are." Another man said, "Hang on to that hat, it will) Tj T*
(be a valuable heir-loom in your family." Still another man in the crowd) Tj T*
(who seemed to envy the possessor of this good fortune, said, "Come, give) Tj T*
