(BE POLITE AND KIND TO YOUR CUSTOMERS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Politeness and civility are the best capital ever invested in business.) Tj T*
(Large stores, gilt signs, flaming advertisements, will all prove) Tj T*
(unavailing if you or your employees treat your patrons abruptly. The) Tj T*
(truth is, the more kind and liberal a man is, the more generous will be) Tj T*
(the patronage bestowed upon him. "Like begets like." The man who gives) Tj T*
(the greatest amount of goods of a corresponding quality for the least) Tj T*
(sum \(still reserving for himself a profit\) will generally succeed best) Tj T*
(in the long run. This brings us to the golden rule, "As ye would that) Tj T*
(men should do to you, do ye also to them" and they will do better by) Tj T*
(you than if you always treated them as if you wanted to get the most) Tj T*
(you could out of them for the least return. Men who drive sharp bargains) Tj T*
(with their customers, acting as if they never expected to see them) Tj T*
(again, will not be mistaken. They will never see them again as) Tj T*
(customers. People don't like to pay and get kicked also.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of the ushers in my Museum once told me he intended to whip a man) Tj T*
(who was in the lecture-room as soon as he came out.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("What for?" I inquired.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Because he said I was no gentleman," replied the usher.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
("Never mind," I replied, "he pays for that, and you will not convince) Tj T*
(him you are a gentleman by whipping him. I cannot afford to lose a) Tj T*
(customer. If you whip him, he will never visit the Museum again, and he) Tj T*
(will induce friends to go with him to other places of amusement instead) Tj T*
(of this, and thus you see, I should be a serious loser.") Tj T*
