(BE CHARITABLE) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of course men should be charitable, because it is a duty and a pleasure.) Tj T*
(But even as a matter of policy, if you possess no higher incentive, you) Tj T*
(will find that the liberal man will command patronage, while the sordid,) Tj T*
(uncharitable miser will be avoided.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Solomon says: "There is that scattereth and yet increaseth; and there is) Tj T*
(that withholdeth more than meet, but it tendeth to poverty." Of course) Tj T*
(the only true charity is that which is from the heart.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The best kind of charity is to help those who are willing to help) Tj T*
(themselves. Promiscuous almsgiving, without inquiring into the) Tj T*
(worthiness of the applicant, is bad in every sense. But to search out) Tj T*
(and quietly assist those who are struggling for themselves, is the kind) Tj T*
(that "scattereth and yet increaseth." But don't fall into the idea that) Tj T*
(some persons practice, of giving a prayer instead of a potato, and) Tj T*
(a benediction instead of bread, to the hungry. It is easier to make) Tj T*
(Christians with full stomachs than empty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DON'T BLAB) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some men have a foolish habit of telling their business secrets. If they) Tj T*
(make money they like to tell their neighbors how it was done. Nothing) Tj T*
(is gained by this, and ofttimes much is lost. Say nothing about your) Tj T*
(profits, your hopes, your expectations, your intentions. And this) Tj T*
(should apply to letters as well as to conversation. Goethe makes) Tj T*
(Mephistophilles say: "Never write a letter nor destroy one." Business) Tj T*
(men must write letters, but they should be careful what they put in) Tj T*
(them. If you are losing money, be specially cautious and not tell of it,) Tj T*
(or you will lose your reputation.) Tj T*
