(PRESERVE YOUR INTEGRITY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is more precious than diamonds or rubies. The old miser said to) Tj T*
(his sons: "Get money; get it honestly if you can, but get money:" This) Tj T*
(advice was not only atrociously wicked, but it was the very essence of) Tj T*
(stupidity: It was as much as to say, "if you find it difficult to obtain) Tj T*
(money honestly, you can easily get it dishonestly. Get it in that way.") Tj T*
(Poor fool! Not to know that the most difficult thing in life is to make) Tj T*
(money dishonestly! Not to know that our prisons are full of men who) Tj T*
(attempted to follow this advice; not to understand that no man can) Tj T*
(be dishonest, without soon being found out, and that when his lack) Tj T*
(of principle is discovered, nearly every avenue to success is closed) Tj T*
(against him forever. The public very properly shun all whose integrity) Tj T*
(is doubted. No matter how polite and pleasant and accommodating a man) Tj T*
(may be, none of us dare to deal with him if we suspect "false weights) Tj T*
(and measures." Strict honesty, not only lies at the foundation of) Tj T*
(all success in life \(financially\), but in every other respect.) Tj T*
(Uncompromising integrity of character is invaluable. It secures to its) Tj T*
(possessor a peace and joy which cannot be attained without it--which no) Tj T*
(amount of money, or houses and lands can purchase. A man who is known) Tj T*
(to be strictly honest, may be ever so poor, but he has the purses of) Tj T*
(all the community at his disposal--for all know that if he promises to) Tj T*
(return what he borrows, he will never disappoint them. As a mere matter) Tj T*
(of selfishness, therefore, if a man had no higher motive for being) Tj T*
(honest, all will find that the maxim of Dr. Franklin can never fail to) Tj T*
(be true, that "honesty is the best policy.") Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To get rich, is not always equivalent to being successful. "There are) Tj T*
(many rich poor men," while there are many others, honest and devout men) Tj T*
(and women, who have never possessed so much money as some rich persons) Tj T*
(squander in a week, but who are nevertheless really richer and happier) Tj T*
(than any man can ever be while he is a transgressor of the higher laws) Tj T*
(of his being.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The inordinate love of money, no doubt, may be and is "the root of all) Tj T*
(evil," but money itself, when properly used, is not only a "handy thing) Tj T*
(to have in the house," but affords the gratification of blessing our) Tj T*
(race by enabling its possessor to enlarge the scope of human happiness) Tj T*
(and human influence. The desire for wealth is nearly universal, and none) Tj T*
(can say it is not laudable, provided the possessor of it accepts its) Tj T*
(responsibilities, and uses it as a friend to humanity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The history of money-getting, which is commerce, is a history of) Tj T*
(civilization, and wherever trade has flourished most, there, too, have) Tj T*
(art and science produced the noblest fruits. In fact, as a general) Tj T*
(thing, money-getters are the benefactors of our race. To them, in a) Tj T*
(great measure, are we indebted for our institutions of learning and of) Tj T*
(art, our academies, colleges and churches. It is no argument against the) Tj T*
(desire for, or the possession of wealth, to say that there are sometimes) Tj T*
(misers who hoard money only for the sake of hoarding and who have no) Tj T*
(higher aspiration than to grasp everything which comes within their) Tj T*
